---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const title = 'About salarynegotiationcalc.com | Free Salary Negotiation Calculator';
const description = 'About salarynegotiationcalc.com — a free compensation strategy resource covering salary negotiation ranges, industry multipliers, anchoring, and total compensation.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>About salarynegotiationcalc.com</h1>
  <p>salarynegotiationcalc.com is a free compensation-strategy resource maintained by The Click Lab. Our Salary Negotiation Calculator and guides help job seekers and working professionals understand their market value, build a defensible negotiation target, and walk into a salary conversation &mdash; a new offer, an annual review, a promotion, or a competing offer &mdash; with a number they can actually justify. This is a career and compensation tool, not a legal-claims resource: nothing here addresses discrimination, retaliation, or equal-pay litigation, and the guidance throughout reflects that.</p>

  <h2>What we do</h2>
  <p>We translate labor-market data &mdash; industry pay multipliers, geographic premiums, and experience-based progression &mdash; into a free calculator and a set of practical negotiation guides. Every page is reviewed by our <a href="/editorial/">editorial team</a> against BLS Occupational Employment Statistics and published commercial salary database research (Glassdoor, Levels.fyi, LinkedIn Salary). The calculator estimates a target range and an opening anchor number by applying industry, location, and experience adjustments to your current salary or an existing offer &mdash; the same categories of data a recruiter or compensation analyst would use informally.</p>

  <h2>Who this calculator is for</h2>
  <p>Most visitors fall into a few groups: job seekers who've received an offer and want to know whether it's fair before accepting or countering; employees preparing for an annual review or promotion conversation who want a data-backed number to anchor on; people juggling multiple competing offers who need a quick way to compare total compensation, not just base salary; and career coaches or recruiters who want a starting benchmark to discuss with a client. We built the Site around these use cases, which is why every guide leads with a plain-English answer before it gets into the research methodology.</p>

  <h2>How the estimate is built</h2>
  <p>The full mechanics &mdash; the industry multiplier table, the location premium tiers, and the experience-based progression curve &mdash; are documented on our <a href="/methodology/">methodology page</a>, including the BLS and commercial salary database sources behind each figure. At a high level: the calculator starts from your current salary or an existing offer, applies an industry multiplier and a location premium, then layers in an experience adjustment with diminishing returns at higher tenure. The result is a target range and a higher opening anchor, meant to be validated against your own research on Glassdoor, Levels.fyi, or LinkedIn Salary before you use it in a real conversation.</p>

  <h2>What we don't do</h2>
  <p>We don't provide personalized career advice or compensation consulting, and we don't evaluate your specific offer, employer, or negotiating position. Actual outcomes depend on factors a calculator can't see: a specific company's pay bands, your particular skill set and track record, internal budget constraints, and how the conversation is actually handled. We also don't recommend specific recruiters, coaches, or employers, and we don't accept referral arrangements with any of them. For guidance on what to do next, see our <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>.</p>

  <h2>Editorial standards</h2>
  <p>Every guide and reference page is written from primary sources &mdash; BLS Occupational Employment Statistics, published commercial salary database methodology, and documented negotiation research &mdash; and reviewed before publication. We don't publish thin content: each page is meant to answer a specific question a job seeker would actually ask before a real conversation, with enough detail to act on. Our full editorial workflow, contributor backgrounds, and sourcing policy are on the <a href="/editorial/">editorial team page</a>.</p>

  <h2>How we make money</h2>
  <p>Display advertising via Google AdSense. We don't sell reader data or accept paid placements, and no recruiter, employer, or career-coaching service pays for placement or a favorable mention anywhere on the Site. See our <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a> for details on data handling.</p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>Questions, corrections, or feedback: see our <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>.</p>
</BaseLayout>
